<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transoxiana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Transoxianan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Transoxiana is the historical name for the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> rivers, roughly corresponding to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and neighbouring areas. Its oasis cities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, were centres of trade and learning on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, a contested part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a>, and a launching point for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transoxiana.jpg" alt="Transoxiana" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transoxiana_8th_century.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chagatai Khan (r. 1227–1242) received lands formerly belonging to the Qara Khitai and the Khwarazmian realm: <b>Transoxiana</b> (much of modern Uzbekistan), the Ili River valley, Kashgaria and the Tarim Basin, and the Semirechye steppe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghaznavid state arose from the military household of the Samanid Empire, the Persian dynasty that ruled Khurasan and <b>Transoxiana</b> from Bukhara.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 458 the king Akhshunwar helped Peroz I take the Sasanian throne from his brother Hormizd III, and by 467 Peroz, with Hephthalite aid, had captured Balaam and ended Kidarite rule in <b>Transoxiana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 893 the Samanid amir Ismail ibn Ahmad captured Talas from them, showing that the Muslim states of <b>Transoxiana</b> could still push the Turkic frontier back.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of the Uzbek nomads later followed Abu'l-Khayr's grandson Muhammad Shaybani south into <b>Transoxiana</b>, and the Kazakhs became masters of most of the Dasht-i Qipchaq, the great steppe north of the Syr Darya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire reached its height under Ala ad-Din Muhammad II (r. 1200–1220), who overthrew the Ghurids in Afghanistan and the Qara Khitai in <b>Transoxiana</b> and ruled from western Iran to the Syr Darya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
